          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. Take a tumbler or a bottle. Measure the length of the curved part of the base of glass or bottle and record it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.136,
            0.883,
            0.177
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. Measure the height of your friend and express it in (i) metres (ii) centimetres and (iii) millimetres."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.187,
            0.885,
            0.311
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8. You are given a coin. Estimate how many coins are required to be placed one after the other lengthwise, without leaving any gap between them, to cover the whole length of the chosen side of a notebook. Verify your estimate by measuring the same side of the notebook and the size of the coin using a 15-cm scale."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.322,
            0.883,
            0.362
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9. Give two examples each for linear, circular and oscillatory motion."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.256,
            0.374,
            0.884,
            0.456
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "10. Observe different objects around you. It is easier to express the lengths of some objects in mm, some in cm and some in m. Make a list of three objects in each category and enter them in the Table 5.6."
